{"title": "Repair Speed Is the Whole Secret", "chapter": "The Repair Crews", "summary": "Across twelve countries the pattern repeated: happy long couples raise hurts quickly and specifically, within days, while the tape is still warm. Unhappy ones archived grievances for anniversaries and ambushes. Clara Whitfield calls it repair speed, and found it predicted marital survival better than compatibility, income or even affection. The skill is unglamorous: name the tear while it is small enough to stitch at the kitchen table, and the roof never needs rebuilding.", "example": "A couple married fifty eight years in Lisbon had a rule from their first year: nothing sleeps twice. A hurt named before the second night got a conversation; after that it got interest. They credit the rule, not romance, for outliving three of their children's marriages.", "action": "Set your repair deadline this week: any hurt gets named within 48 hours, kindly, specifically, once. No archives, no ambushes."},
{"title": "Small Doses Beat Grand Gestures", "chapter": "The Daily Dose", "summary": "The lasting couples were almost disappointing in their romance: tea made without asking, a hand on a shoulder passing in a corridor, the good biscuits bought because Tuesday. Grand gestures appeared in the divorced couples' stories, as compensations. Clara Whitfield's dose finding matches the laboratory research: frequent small deposits keep the emotional account solvent, and solvency is what survives the large withdrawals that every long life eventually presents.", "example": "An Ohio farmer expressed fifty years of love in a sentence his wife quoted to Clara Whitfield: he always warmed her side of the bed in winter with a hot water bottle before she came up. She reported this with the gravity other women reserve for diamonds, because frequency outranks size in the arithmetic of being cherished.", "action": "Administer three small doses daily for two weeks: one practical, one affectionate, one appreciative. Track nothing; let your partner's weather report the results."},
{"title": "Protect Their Dignity Like Your Own", "chapter": "In Public and In Private", "summary": "The fifty-year couples never corrected, mocked or outshone each other in company. Private disagreement was universal; public diminishment was absent, and Clara Whitfield came to see it as structural: a partner whose dignity you defend in public trusts you with their dignity in private, including their fears and their ageing body. The couples who performed contempt for laughs were, in her sample, rehearsing the divorce announcement to an audience.", "example": "A Sydney husband famous for wit never once made his wife the joke in forty years of dinners. Asked why, he looked puzzled: she has to live in the room after I get the laugh. His wife, listening, supplied the marriage's epitaph in advance: he made every room safe for me.", "action": "Audit your last month of company talk: one joke, correction or story at your partner's expense gets retired and privately apologised for this week."}
